# AI MLE Employee: Technical Responsibilities, Required Skills & Tooling Ecosystem

## Executive Summary

This research synthesizes Atlassian-internal documentation, job descriptions (P40–P80IC ladder), ML platform architecture pages, codebase analyses, and industry context to comprehensively define what an AI MLE (Machine Learning Engineer) employee does, what skills it needs, and what tooling ecosystem it operates within. The findings are grounded in Atlassian's actual ML infrastructure and practices, making them directly actionable for designing an autonomous MLE agent.

---

## 1. Core MLE Responsibilities: The Model Development Lifecycle

### 1.1 Day-to-Day Responsibilities

Based on multiple Atlassian MLE job descriptions (P40–P80) and internal documentation:

| Phase | MLE Responsibilities |
|-------|---------------------|
| **Problem Framing** | Formulate the ML problem end-to-end; identify feasibility and usability validation techniques based on product requirements; translate business objectives into ML objectives |
| **Data Preparation** | Access and prepare ML datasets (via Socrates/Plato data lakes); curate and collate validation datasets; work with UGC-compliant data access pipelines; define data contracts |
| **Feature Engineering** | Define feature views in Tecton/MLFS; build online and offline features; manage feature transformations (PySpark, Pandas, SQL); ensure training-serving consistency |
| **Model Training** | Design system and model architectures; conduct rigorous experimentation; leverage distributed training (PyTorch DDP, multi-GPU/TPU); use ML Fabric for GPU-accelerated training |
| **Hyperparameter Tuning** | Track experiments via MLflow/Weights & Biases; use Ray for distributed hyperparameter search; iterate on model configurations |
| **Evaluation** | Conduct offline evaluations; benchmark performance against existing models; use ML Lens for observability; compute metrics (precision, recall, SHAP, LIME) |
| **Deployment** | Register models in Tarot/ML Registry; deploy via SageMaker or TEAMServe (Triton); manage service descriptors for Micros; handle model versioning and progressive rollout |
| **Monitoring** | Monitor feature drift (ML Lens,EvidentlyAI); track model performance in production; set up alerting for degradation; manage model retraining triggers |

### 1.2 End-to-End ML Lifecycle at Atlassian

The standard path (FY25–26) as documented in the [End-to-End ML Framework @ Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202046686c7c93274f379403a2283d58293f/pages/6708596091/End-to-End+ML+Framework+Atlassian):

1. **Data Lake & Feature Stores** → Socrates tables, Tecton/MLFS feature views
2. **Training & Experimentation** → Databricks notebooks, MLflow experiments and runs
3. **Workflow Orchestration (ML Studio)** → Workflows as DAGs of modules (data prep, training, evaluation, packaging, registration)
4. **Model Registry & Serving Control Plane** → MSP / Tarot v3 model registry; ML Registry for lineage; Deployment Store for artifact replication
5. **Serving Backends** → SageMaker endpoints and TEAMServe Triton instances
6. **Product Services (Micros)** → Service descriptors referencing MSP model groups/versions
7. **Users and Clients** → Product APIs invoking ML endpoints

### 1.3 Specific Technical Tasks (from MLE User Stories)

From the [MLFS User Experience Stories](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6105442197/MLFS+User+Experience+Stories):

- **MLE-1**: Take a feature definition from a data scientist and productionize it with minimal friction
- **MLE-2**: Follow git-based review workflows (PRGB) for auditability
- **MLE-3**: Implement automated validation in CI/CD to catch errors before deployment
- **MLE-4**: Safely roll out new features with progressive deployment (dogfooding → canary → full release)
- **MLE-5**: Set up automatic monitoring and alerting for feature health metrics (freshness, distribution, drift, null-rate)
- **MLE-6**: Link feature services to model artifacts
- **MLE-7**: Ensure reliable materialization (schedules, online/offline consistency, feature freshness)

---

## 2. Required Technical Skills & Competency Levels

### 2.1 Core Technical Competencies (from Atlassian JDs)

**Mandatory (Day-1expectations):**

| Skill Area | Required Competency |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **Python** | Production-quality code; primary language for ML |
| **ML Frameworks** | PyTorch (dominant,12x growth2021–2025), TensorFlow, HuggingFace Transformers, JAX |
| **Classical ML** | Scikit-learn, XGBoost, Random Forest, SVM |
| **Deep Learning** | BERT, GPT-family, encoder/decoder architectures, attention mechanisms |
| **SQL** | Complex queries on data lakes (Socrates/Databricks) |
| **Cloud/Big Data** | AWS services, Spark, Databricks environments |
| **End-to-end ML** | Problem formulation → experimentation → pipeline design → model serving APIs → deployment |
| **LLM Skills** | Prompt engineering, RAG, fine-tuning (LoRA, full), RLHF/GRPO |
| **Statistical Foundations** | Hypothesis testing, A/B experimentation, causal inference |

**Advanced (P50–P60+ differentiation):**

| Skill Area | Advanced Competency |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **Distributed Training** | PyTorch DDP, DeepSpeed, multi-node GPU training, FSDP |
| **Model Optimization** | Quantization, mixed precision (bf16), ONNX, model compression |
| **MLOps** | CI/CD for ML, continuous training (CT), automated retraining |
| **System Architecture** | 70% engineering / 30% ML split (per P60 JD); microservices, REST APIs |
| **Serving Infrastructure** | Triton Inference Server, vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SageMaker |
| **GPU Programming** | CUDA awareness, GPU utilization optimization |
| **Feature Engineering at Scale** | Feature stores, online/offline serving, training-serving skew prevention |

### 2.2 Market-Validated Skills (from TechWolf Analysis)

From [AI skill market data](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120207b67d459f263479392b6ccf6b9271069/pages/6671530707/Questions+for+TechWolf+Agent):

| Skill |2025 Market Share | Growth2021→2025 |
|-------|------------------|------------------|
| Machine Learning | 1.86% | 1.9x |
| TensorFlow | 1.02% | 6.5x |
| PyTorch | 0.96% | 12.2x |
| **MLOps** | 0.63% | **24.1x** 🚀 |
| Deep Learning | 0.62% | 5.4x |
| Prompt Engineering | 0.48% | ∞ (new) |

Key insight: **PyTorch has overtaken TensorFlow** (12x growth vs 6.5x). **MLOps is the fastest riser** (24x growth), indicating industrialization of ML is a top priority.

### 2.3 GenAI Upskilling Gap

From [GenAI Readiness Assessment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/pages/viewpageattachments.action?pageId=6671530707&preview=%2F6671530707%2F6693162051%2FPV+Q4.pdf):
- Only 2/98 ML Engineering + Data Science employees have explicit GenAI skills on their profile
- Target skill set: Generative AI, Large Language Models, Prompt Engineering, Transformer Models, Deep Learning, PyTorch, TensorFlow, Neural Networks, Model Development, NLP
- Biggest gap: GenAI appears in 11.7% of Atlassian's own market postings but zero internal profiles include it

---

## 3. MLOps Tooling Ecosystem (Atlassian-Specific)

### 3.1Atlassian ML Platform Stack

From [ML Platforms @ Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020f8645f9b46414d32962ab94d66c30436/pages/6068577839/ML+Platforms+Atlassian):

| Component | Tool/Platform | Purpose |
|-----------|-------------|---------|
| **ML Platform (MLP)** | Atlassian's core internal platform | End-to-end tooling for developing, deploying, and operating ML models |
| **ML Studio** | Databricks-based collaborative environment | Model development, experimentation, data analysis; monorepo with ~1,550 modules, ~3,200 workflows |
| **Tarot (Model Registry)** | Atlassian model registry service | Centralizing metadata, versioning, and deployment info for ML models |
| **Model Serving Platform (MSP)** | Abstraction over AWS SageMaker | Deploying ML models as containerized inference endpoints |
| **TEAMServe** | In-house Triton-based serving | gRPC-based model inference (~59% cost savings over SageMaker) |
| **Tecton** | Feature engineering platform (3rd party) | Creation, storage, and retrieval of reusable features; ~400 feature views, ~20K req/sec |
| **MLFS** | Atlassian's in-house feature store | Replacing Tecton; UGC-compliant, low-latency, cost-optimized feature serving |
| **MLflow** | Experiment tracking (integrated with Databricks) | Tracking experiments, model training runs, artifact management |
| **ML Fabric** | Kubernetes-based training infrastructure | Multi-node distributed training, GPU clusters (H200), Ray workloads |
| **ML Lens** | Platformized ML observability | Feature drift detection, prediction drift, model latency/error alerting |
| **Praxis ML** | Unified training framework | Standardized base classes, recipes, CLI for training/evaluation/deployment |
| **Analytics Platform** | Databricks workspace (vNext Data Lake) | Data access, analytics, ML experimentation |
| **Launchpad** | Model testing/deployment | Find a model → test on GPU cluster → progressive rollout |

### 3.2 Experiment Tracking & Model Management

- **MLflow** (primary): Integrated with Databricks for tracking training runs, model experiments, artifact management
- **Weights & Biases**: Integrated with Praxis ML for experiment tracking and visualization
- **ML Registry / Tarot v3**: Central registry with data classification labels, UGC compliance, deployment store
- **Deployment Store**: Artifact replication across regions/environments

### 3.3 Orchestration

- **ML Studio Orchestrator**: Workflow DAGs composed of modules; YAML-based workflow descriptors
- **ML Fabric (Kubeflow/Kueue)**: Kubernetes-native job scheduling for GPU training workloads
- **Bitbucket Pipelines**: CI/CD for module publishing, validation, and deployment

### 3.4 Model Serving

- **TEAMServe**: gRPC-based Triton endpoints; migrating from SageMaker (~$5.6M/year savings)
- **SageMaker**: Real-time inference endpoints; being phased out in favor of TEAMServe
- **Prediction Sidecar**: Micros sidecar for embedded model serving (legacy, limited to MLeap models)
- **AI Gateway**: Routing to different model providers (OpenAI, Google, MSP, Bedrock, Fireworks AI)

### 3.5 Monitoring & Observability

- **ML Lens**: Platform-level drift detection, feature freshness monitoring, serving observability
- **EvidentlyAI**: Open-source ML monitoring for feature drift and re-ranking metrics
- **SignalFx/Splunk**: Infrastructure monitoring, detectors-as-code
- **Custom dashboards**: Feature quality (drift, null-rate, distribution), model latency, error rates

---

## 4. Infrastructure & Platform Skills

### 4.1 Cloud ML Services

| Cloud | Services Used |
|-------|--------------|
| **AWS** | SageMaker (inference), S3 (storage), EKS (Kubernetes), Lambda, SQS, CloudFormation |
| **GCP** | GKE (Kubernetes), Managed Lustre (high-performance distributed training storage) |
| **Multi-cloud** | TEAMServe deployed across AWS and GCP; ML Fabric supports both |

### 4.2 Containerization & GPU Cluster Management

- **Docker**: Base images (python:3.12.0), module packaging
- **Kubernetes (EKS/GKE)**: ML Fabric orchestration; PyTorchJob CRDs; Kueue for batch scheduling
- **GPU Instances**: H200 (141GB×8), A100 (40GB/80GB×8) for training
- **Lustre**: High-performance shared filesystem for multi-node training (addressing I/O bottleneck at scale)

### 4.3 CI/CD for ML

From [ML Studio Architecture](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CAAS/pages/6811181014/ML+Studio+Architecture):
- **PRGB (Peer-Reviewed Green Build)**: Quality control for production code
- **Bitbucket Pipelines**: Automated build → validate → publish → deploy
- **Module versioning**: `:latestAlpha` for testing, `:latest` for stable, pinned versions for production
- **SOX/non-SOX profiles**: Compliance-aware deployment pipelines
- **CODEOWNERS**: Team-based code ownership rules

### 4.4 Data Infrastructure

- **Socrates**: Atlassian's data lake (production tables)
- **Databricks**: ML workspaces for training and experimentation
- **Plato**: UGC data access with privacy controls
- **Delta Lake**: Table format for data versioning
- **vNext Data Lake**: Next-generation platformized data lake

