## 6. Role Boundaries: MLE vs Adjacent Roles

### 6.1 Role Family Taxonomy at Atlassian

From [Which AI roles have we hired at Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~788312608/pages/6993940216/Which+AI+roles+have+we+hired+at+Atlassian):

1. **Machine Learning Engineer (P40–P80)** — End-to-end model development, training, deployment
2. **Machine Learning Scientist / Principal ML Scientist** — Forecasting, recommendations, growth, LLM research
3. **Machine Learning Systems Engineer (MSE)** — Infrastructure, pipelines, productionization (70% engineering / 30% ML)
4. **AI Forward Deployed Engineer (AI FDE)** — Solution architecture with strategic customers
5. **ML/AI Engineering Managers & Heads** — Leadership ladder (M60–M80)

### 6.2 Scope Boundaries

| Role | Primary Focus | Distinguishing Characteristic |
|------|--------------|------------------------------|
| **MLE** | End-to-end model lifecycle: problem framing → training → deployment → monitoring | Owns model quality AND production deployment |
| **Data Scientist (DS)** | Experimentation, feature prototyping, offline evaluation, model training; hands off production to MLE | Does NOT own production deployment |
| **Data Engineer (DE)** | Data pipelines, logging instrumentation, table management; productionalizes DS prototypes | Focuses on data flow, not model logic |
| **ML Systems Engineer (MSE)** | Core infrastructure that enables MLEs: training platforms, serving infrastructure, registries | Builds the platform, not the models |
| **ML Platform Engineer** | Same as MSE — builds the "rails" (ML Studio, TEAMServe, MLFS, ML Lens) | Never builds product-facing models |
| **Research Scientist** | Novel algorithms, publications, frontier exploration | Publishes papers; less production focus |

From [MLFS User Experience Stories](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6105442197/MLFS+User+Experience+Stories):
> "These are personas, not people. These functions could be performed by the same person wearing different hats."
- **DS**: Data analysis, feature prototyping, rapid iteration, offline evaluation, model training
- **MLE**: Productionizing features, deployment, monitoring, rollout safety
- **DE**: Feature prototyping, productionizing features, monitoring (combination of DS + MLE who specializes in data → features → prod)

### 6.3 The MLE Sweet Spot

From the [P60 Principal MLSE JD](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SER/pages/6151865844/P43900+-+P60+Principal+Machine+Learning+System+Engineer): "This job requires strong software engineering skills: **70% engineering and 30% machine learning.**"

However, for pure **MLE** (vs MSE) the ratio inverts — MLEs spend more time on model development while still owning production:
- Design model architectures
- Conduct experimentation and evaluation
- Build production-grade training pipelines
- Deploy and monitor models in production
- Collaborate with DS for prototyping and with MSE/Platform for infrastructure needs

