## 5. ML Engineering Codebases: Architectural Patterns

### 5.1 ML Studio: The Canonical Monorepo

ML Studio is Atlassian's **primary ML codebase** — a multi-tenant monorepo with the following structure:

```
ml-studio/
├── modules/src/<team>/<module>/     # ~1,550 modules across 65teams
│   ├── module_descriptor.yaml# Inputs/outputs, metadata
│   ├── src/                         # Module code
│   ├── included/scripts/            # Entrypoint scripts
│   └── pyproject.toml              # Dependencies
├── workflows/src/<team>/*.yaml      # ~3,200 workflow definitions
├── libraries/src/<team>/<lib>/      # ~72 shared libraries across23 teams
├── admin/                # CI orchestration (Python)
│   ├── main.py                     # Main orchestrator
│   ├── module_validator.py         # Validation
│   ├── pipeline_orchestrator.py    # Bitbucket API
│   └── test/# 394 tests
├── bin/                            # Shell scripts for CI/CD
├── alerts/                         # ML Lens alert rules
├── .bitbucket/CODEOWNERS           # Team ownership
├── .bitbucket/teams.yml            # 50+ team definitions
└── bitbucket-pipelines.yml         # CI/CD configuration
```

### 5.2 Key Architectural Patterns

| Pattern | Description | Example |
|---------|-------------|---------|
| **Three-layer separation** | Libraries (reusable logic) → Modules (execution units) → Workflows (DAG orchestration) | Contextual Bandits migration |
| **Module contracts** | `module_descriptor.yaml` defines inputs/outputs (consumes/produces) for dependency tracking | All ML Studio modules |
| **Global config injection** | Eliminates deep constructor parameter passing; workflow parameters injected at runtime | `training_and_registration_config.py` |
| **Bridge/Adapter pattern** | Inject ML Studio workflow parameters into library code | `MLStudioAdapter`, `RewardProcessor` |
| **Workflow-type-aware isolation** | `RELEASED` = production; `EXPERIMENTAL`/`ADHOC` = `-dev` suffix (prevents Tarot overwrites) | Model group naming |
| **Idempotent table/model group creation** | Modules create output tables and model groups on first run; no manual setup | All modules |
| **Platform override** | Workflow tasks can specify `platform_override: "fabric"` for GPU training | LLM fine-tuning workflows |

### 5.3 Training Pipeline Organization

From [ML Fabrics Training Infrastructure](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63d0e8ba8dd199a03e115452/pages/6604046821/ML+Fabrics+Training+Infrastructure+-+Code+Analysis):

```
modules/src/ml_platform/ml_fabric_create_run/
├── main.py           # Orchestration: Databricks setup, K8s secrets, job submission
├── kitt.py           # Kubernetes interface: PyTorchJob/RayJob creation
├── databricks.py     # Databricks integration: tokens, service principals
└── job.py            # Job monitoring: polling, Splunk links, state transitions

modules/src/ml_platform/mlp_llm_finetune/
├── included/scripts/entrypoint.py  # MLflow setup, model download, torchrun, artifact upload
└── module_descriptor.yaml          # Inputs: base_model_uri, data_uri, training_config
```

### 5.4 Model Serving Patterns

- **MSP model groups**: Versioned model identifiers referenced in service descriptors
- **Micros service descriptors** (`*.sd.yml`): Declare SageMaker or TEAMServe endpoints
- **Feature flags for migration**: e.g., `platform_enable_teamserve_streaming` for progressive rollout
- **Shadow testing**: Run new serving path in parallel with existing (e.g., `similar_issues_teamserve_rerank_shadow`)

### 5.5 Configuration Management

- **Workflow YAML descriptors**: Define module DAG, compute requirements, scheduling
- **Module descriptors**: YAML defining inputs/outputs, version, team ownership
- **Feature view definitions**: Python/YAML in Tecton or MLFS
- **Infrastructure-as-Code**: Terraform for SignalFx dashboards/detectors; SLO encoding in IaC

---

## 6. Role Boundaries: MLE vs Adjacent Roles

### 6.1 Role Family Taxonomy at Atlassian

From [Which AI roles have we hired at Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~788312608/pages/6993940216/Which+AI+roles+have+we+hired+at+Atlassian):

1. **Machine Learning Engineer (P40–P80)** — End-to-end model development, training, deployment
2. **Machine Learning Scientist / Principal ML Scientist** — Forecasting, recommendations, growth, LLM research
3. **Machine Learning Systems Engineer (MSE)** — Infrastructure, pipelines, productionization (70% engineering / 30% ML)
4. **AI Forward Deployed Engineer (AI FDE)** — Solution architecture with strategic customers
5. **ML/AI Engineering Managers & Heads** — Leadership ladder (M60–M80)

### 6.2 Scope Boundaries

| Role | Primary Focus | Distinguishing Characteristic |
|------|--------------|------------------------------|
| **MLE** | End-to-end model lifecycle: problem framing → training → deployment → monitoring | Owns model quality AND production deployment |
| **Data Scientist (DS)** | Experimentation, feature prototyping, offline evaluation, model training; hands off production to MLE | Does NOT own production deployment |
| **Data Engineer (DE)** | Data pipelines, logging instrumentation, table management; productionalizes DS prototypes | Focuses on data flow, not model logic |
| **ML Systems Engineer (MSE)** | Core infrastructure that enables MLEs: training platforms, serving infrastructure, registries | Builds the platform, not the models |
| **ML Platform Engineer** | Same as MSE — builds the "rails" (ML Studio, TEAMServe, MLFS, ML Lens) | Never builds product-facing models |
| **Research Scientist** | Novel algorithms, publications, frontier exploration | Publishes papers; less production focus |

From [MLFS User Experience Stories](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6105442197/MLFS+User+Experience+Stories):
> "These are personas, not people. These functions could be performed by the same person wearing different hats."
- **DS**: Data analysis, feature prototyping, rapid iteration, offline evaluation, model training
- **MLE**: Productionizing features, deployment, monitoring, rollout safety
- **DE**: Feature prototyping, productionizing features, monitoring (combination of DS + MLE who specializes in data → features → prod)

### 6.3 The MLE Sweet Spot

From the [P60 Principal MLSE JD](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SER/pages/6151865844/P43900+-+P60+Principal+Machine+Learning+System+Engineer): "This job requires strong software engineering skills: **70% engineering and 30% machine learning.**"

However, for pure **MLE** (vs MSE) the ratio inverts — MLEs spend more time on model development while still owning production:
- Design model architectures
- Conduct experimentation and evaluation
- Build production-grade training pipelines
- Deploy and monitor models in production
- Collaborate with DS for prototyping and with MSE/Platform for infrastructure needs

---

## 7. Key Findings & Implications for an Autonomous MLE Agent

### 7.1 Core Workflow Loops an MLE Agent Must Execute

1. **Experiment Loop**: Access data → define features → train model → evaluate → iterate
2. **Productionization Loop**: Package model → register in Tarot → deploy to TEAMServe/SageMaker → validate
3. **Monitoring Loop**: Check drift metrics → assess model degradation → trigger retraining if needed
4. **MLOps Loop**: Manage CI/CD pipelines → validate module changes → progressive rollout

### 7.2 Tooling the Agent Must Interface With

| Category | Tools |
|----------|-------|
| **Data Access** | Databricks/Socrates, Plato (UGC), Delta tables |
| **Feature Engineering** | Tecton, MLFS, PySpark, Pandas |
| **Training** | ML Studio (modules/workflows), ML Fabric (Kubernetes), Praxis ML |
| **Experiment Tracking** | MLflow, Weights & Biases |
| **Model Registry** | Tarot v3, ML Registry |
| **Serving** | TEAMServe (Triton gRPC), SageMaker, AI Gateway |
| **Observability** | ML Lens, EvidentlyAI, SignalFx, Splunk |
| **CI/CD** | Bitbucket Pipelines, PRGB workflows |
| **Orchestration** | ML Studio Orchestrator, Kubeflow, Kueue |

### 7.3 Critical Technical Competencies for the Agent

1. **Python fluency** — All ML development at Atlassian is Python-first
2. **PyTorch mastery** — Dominant framework (DDP, model architecture, GPU optimization)
3. **MLflow proficiency** — Experiment tracking, model registry, artifact management
4. **Kubernetes awareness** — ML Fabric, PyTorchJob CRDs, resource scheduling
5. **YAML workflow authoring** — ML Studio module/workflow descriptors
6. **Feature store operations** — Tecton/MLFS feature view definition and management
7. **Model deployment** — TEAMServe/SageMaker registration, service descriptor configuration
8. **Observability** — Drift detection, alerting setup, metric monitoring

