## 2. Domain Operational Artifacts

### 2.1 Model Cards

From [AI fairness & data risks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121eeecde7086e94a778d189b8b5d0a90f2/pages/5749449742/AI+fairness+data+risks), model cards should document:

- **Purpose**: One paragraph on what the model does and who benefits
- **Data sources**: Training data provenance, rights, and known skews
- **Performance**: Metrics sliced by group (region, cohort), including FP/FN by segment
- **Limitations**: Known failure modes, OOD behavior, bias results
- **Fairness results**: Group-level performance breakdowns, mitigation methods applied
- **Rollback plan**: How to pause, roll back, or retire

### 2.2 LLM Evaluation Reports

Atlassian has a standardized [LLM Evaluation Report Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ler/pages/5204201088/LLM+Evaluation+Report+Template):

```
Report Title:<Use Case>-<LLM Model Name>-Evaluation Report

Sections:
1. Basic Information (Product, Use Case ID, LLM in production, LLM in evaluation, Status, DRI)
2. Executive Summary- Go Production Recommendation: Go / No Go
   - Quality Eval Results: Notable better / On par / Notable worse / Mixed
   - Latency Eval Results: Notable better / On par / Notable worse / Mixed
   - Cost Eval Results: Notable better / On par / Notable worse / Mixed
3. Quality Summary (with doc links)
4. Latency Summary (with doc links)
5. Cost Summary (with doc links)
```

Reporting process ([How to report LLM Evaluation Results](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ler/pages/5327693188/How+to+report+LLM+Evaluation+Results)):
1. Draft evaluation report in Confluence under the LLM Evaluation workspace
2. Organize under folders categorized by model name
3. Copy executive summary to centralized aggregation doc for LT visibility

### 2.3 Model Monitoring Dashboards

From [MLOPs@CoreML Long Term Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202089f42605e1d342df8e8011fe153d216f/pages/5783505356/MLOPs+CoreML+Long+Term+Plan) (Level 3: Monitoring & Observability):

| Category | Metrics |
|----------|---------|
| ML Metrics (Model Health) | Prediction distributions, feature distributions & data quality, offline performance (accuracy, precision/recall), online performance (engagement, adoption, retention) |
| Operational Metrics (System Health) | Latency, timeout rate, resource utilization (CPU, GPU, memory, disk), training/inference cost, pipeline failures |
| Dashboards | Unified dashboard with operational + ML metrics + cost |
| Alerting | Latency > threshold, drift score > threshold, pipeline failures |

### 2.4 Incident Response Runbooks for Model Degradation

From [ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/6058351626/ML-Engine+Circuit+Breaker+Runbook) and [MLFS Incident Priorities and Hotfix Process](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5749098244/Runbook+MLFS+Incident+Priorities+and+Hotfix+Process):

**Runbook structure:**
1. Validate alert context in monitoring tools
2. Inspect error rates, top error codes, impacted endpoints/models
3. Check downstream health (latency, saturation, error budgets)
4. If high impact confirmed: consider disabling via feature gate with documented rationale
5. For tuning/safe changes: propose change → canary 5-10% → monitor2 windows → ramp to 100%

### 2.5 A/B Test Analysis Templates

From internal experiment design patterns:
- Control vs. treatment definition
- L1 changes (system-level) and L2 changes (model-level) documented
- Guardrail metrics with tolerance levels
- Success criteria and rollout strategy
- Bake-in time requirements
- Online metrics computed from events, propagated to experiment platform (e.g., Statsig)

