## 7. Model Governance and Compliance

### 7.1 Model Documentation Standards

From [The Forms of an ML Training Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3229221104/The+Forms+of+an+ML+Training+Experiment):

**Data Governance Requirements:**
- Training components require clear owner and approved training purposes
- Model artifacts categorized by data category with access restrictions
- Some artifacts must be data-resident if weights constitute UGC
- Data use policy compliance required for all training on customer data

**System Integration Documentation:**
- Public model registry access (downloading base models)
- Internal model registries (publishing artifacts)
- Data catalogs (consuming training sets)
- Compute requirements and infrastructure dependencies

### 7.2 Model Registry and Versioning

From [Model Deployments Now in ML Registry & Lineage](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/blog/2026/01/15/6317041646/Model+Deployments+Now+in+ML+Registry+Lineage):

- **Model Registry (Tarot v3)**: All model artifacts, configs, and dependencies versioned and retrievable
- **Deployment tracking**: TEAMServe and Launchpad deployments tracked in registry
- **Lineage Graph**: Full traceability from model → deployment version → infrastructure config
- **Deployment versions**: Capture detailed deployment config as artifacts within component versions
- **Rollback**: Simple version tag change in `.sd.yml`, redeploy to previous version

### 7.3 Data Privacy and Retention

From [ML Studio - Compliance Improvements for Per-tenant Modeling](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-102262):

- **Raw UGC deletion**: All datasets containing Raw UGC must be deleted within 30 days of creation
- **Automatic artifact deletion**: ML Studio enforces automatic deletion of tables and model artifacts associated with Raw UGC
- **Data residency**: Model artifacts with UGC weights stored in data-resident locations
- **Access controls**: Schema-level access controls per use case and region

### 7.4 Responsible AI Checks

From [AI energy use & ethics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121eeecde7086e94a778d189b8b5d0a90f2/pages/5749356103/AI+energy+use+ethics):

**Simple guardrails checklist:**
- [ ] Purpose & users documented in one paragraph
- [ ] Data rights & privacy checked; retention limits set
- [ ] Sensitive attributes excluded or justified and reviewed
- [ ] Baseline performance (non-AI) defined and beaten
- [ ] Fairness checks across key groups
- [ ] Human-in-the-loop in place for high-impact decisions
- [ ] Safety tuning and abuse filters validated
- [ ] Monitoring for quality, drift, and incidents
- [ ] Audit logs enabled; access minimised
- [ ] Sunset plan (how to pause, roll back, or retire)

### 7.5 Approval Workflows

From [MLOPs@CoreML Long Term Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202089f42605e1d342df8e8011fe153d216f/pages/5783505356/MLOPs+CoreML+Long+Term+Plan):

**Model Registry Stages:**
1. **Staging**: Models from experimentation
2. **Review**: QA/peer validation and/or automated tests (unit tests, performance benchmarks)
3. **Approved**: Authorized for production deployment
4. **Integration with CI/CD**: Approval triggers automated deployment pipelines

### 7.6 Audit Trail Requirements

From operational patterns and governance documentation:

- Every model deployment version captures deployment config as artifacts
- ML Registry maintains deployment records for auditing
- Model identified and audited via config in code (model name + commit hash)
- Prediction logs include: timestamp, user ID, model version, score, and config
- All policy checks, model calls, and overrides logged for observability
- Feature flag changes tracked with rationale documentation

